  "regional": "Belgium and Switzerland say septante (70) and nonante (90). Some Swiss cantons say huitante (80). In France: soixante-dix, quatre-vingts, quatre-vingt-dix.",
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "subtitle": "From 0 to 1000. 21, 31, 41, 51, 61 and 71 use et: vingt et un, soixante et onze, but vingt-deux. 70 is soixante-dix, 80 is quatre-vingts (4 × 20) and 90 is quatre-vingt-dix. Cent and vingt take an -s only at the end: deux cents, but deux cent trois.",
  "briefMarks": ["seize", "vingt et un", "soixante-dix", "quatre-vingts", "quatre-vingt-dix", "cent", "mille"],
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "0–16: learn them", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "17–69: tens + ones", "variant": "verb" },
      { "text": "70–99: soixante-dix, quatre-vingts" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "16 → seize · 21 → vingt et un · 22 → vingt-deux · 71 → soixante et onze · 80 → quatre-vingts · 91 → quatre-vingt-onze · 200 → deux cents",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>0 to 16</strong> are single words you have to learn: <em>zéro, un, deux, trois, quatre, cinq, six, sept, huit, neuf, dix, onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize</em>. Then <em>dix-sept, dix-huit, dix-neuf</em>.",
        "tag": "0–19"
      },
      {
        "html": "Tens: <em>vingt, trente, quarante, cinquante, soixante</em>. Add the ones with a hyphen: <em>vingt-deux, trente-cinq, quarante-huit</em>. For 21, 31, 41, 51 and 61 use <strong>et</strong> and no hyphen: <em>vingt et un, trente et un</em>. Before a noun it agrees: <em>vingt et un ans, vingt et une personnes</em>.",
        "tag": "20–69"
      },
      {
        "html": "70 to 99 count on from 60 and 80. <em>70 soixante-dix, 71 soixante et onze, 72 soixante-douze</em>. <em>80 quatre-vingts</em> (4 × 20), <em>81 quatre-vingt-un</em> (no et), <em>90 quatre-vingt-dix, 91 quatre-vingt-onze</em>.",
        "tag": "70–99"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>cent</strong> = 100. <em>deux cents</em> (200) has an -s because nothing follows. With more numbers there is no -s: <em>deux cent trois</em>. Same rule as <em>quatre-vingts</em> but <em>quatre-vingt-deux</em>. <strong>mille</strong> (1000) never takes an -s: <em>deux mille</em>.",
        "tag": "cent, mille"
      },
      {
        "html": "A comma marks the decimals, a space marks the thousands: <em>12,50 €</em> is <em>douze euros cinquante</em>, and <em>1 250 €</em> is <em>mille deux cent cinquante euros</em>. From two, <em>euros</em> has an -s: <em>un euro, deux euros</em>.",
        "tag": "Prices"
      },
      {
        "html": "A phone number has 10 digits and is said in pairs: <em>06 11 22 33 44</em> → <em>zéro six, onze, vingt-deux, trente-trois, quarante-quatre</em>. You can always say it digit by digit.",
        "tag": "Phone numbers"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>vingt-un</em> → ✓ <em>vingt et un</em>. ✗ <em>quatre-vingt euros</em> → ✓ <em>quatre-vingts euros</em>. ✗ <em>deux cent euros</em> → ✓ <em>deux cents euros</em>. Since 1990 the spelling with hyphens everywhere (<em>vingt-et-un, deux-cent-trois</em>) is also correct. Use numbers with <a href=\"/temps-et-jours\">the time and days</a>.",
